parse.js is a rewrite of marked's lexer, tokenizer, renderer and parser, specialized to marked's default
options (gfm on, breaks/pedantic/async off, no extensions or hooks). Its parse(markdown) is meant to return
exactly what marked.parse(markdown) returns. What changed relative to marked:

- Options, extension, hook and walkTokens plumbing removed; only the gfm grammar is kept. The block and
  inline regexes are still composed at load time with marked's own edit()/caret logic, so their sources
  match marked's.
- Lists: the first line is found with indexOf/slice, and tab expansion is skipped on lines without tabs.
- Inline lexing: tokenizers are dispatched on the first character; a source with no inline syntax becomes
  a single text token directly; reduced text regexes are used when no '@', ':' or 'www.' can make the
  email/URL branches match; the emphasis mask skips masking steps that cannot change anything; Unicode
  spread arrays in emphasis handling are replaced by direct code-point helpers.
- The emphasis mask and closer indexes are built lazily, only when emphasis or strikethrough is reached.
- Setext headings: the lheading regex runs only if a candidate underline line exists before the first
  blank line.
- The inline lexer and renderer are fused: inline lexing produces HTML directly (adjacent text runs are
  buffered and escaped once), and image alt text comes from a plain-text mode of the same pass.
- Block rules are tried only when the first character (after up to 3 spaces) can start them; the table
  rule only when the second line can be a delimiter row.
- Links: cleanUrl skips encodeURI for URLs made only of characters it leaves unchanged, and backslash
  unescaping runs only when a backslash is present.

main.ts is new: it reads /input/cases.json, renders each case with parse(), and writes /output/html.json.
